1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,851
The evidence meeting had a different shape since the survey had been rewritten.

2
00:00:03,851 --> 00:00:12,150
One measure replaced twelve: whether a person could find, in one minute, the thing they came for.

3
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:18,714
“When the number is smaller than the argument, the argument wins,” Maya said.

4
00:00:18,714 --> 00:00:24,603
“Until we can stay small and honest, we should not grow.”

5
00:00:24,603 --> 00:00:37,711
They pinned a chart to the retreat wall and compared two households in the southwest: one with a car and a reliable piano teacher, one with a dependent grandmother and four bicycles.

6
00:00:37,711 --> 00:00:42,630
The rate of attendance was the same; the sadness was not.

7
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:47,630
“Apparently that is called a clash of measures,” Lin wrote.

8
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:51,564
“Really it is a clash of lives.”

9
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:59,583
A parental question on the committee asked whether the project could remain neutral, and the verbal answer was easy.

10
00:00:59,583 --> 00:01:05,532
“Communication is not a crowd,” the enthusiast with the violin said.

11
00:01:05,532 --> 00:01:18,300
“It is a dialog.” Her singular habit of speaking in the present tense had already changed the room, and the change involved the status of every volunteer.

12
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:24,759
A skinny boy in worn clothing brought a pillow and a pencil to the gathering and asked to stay.

13
00:01:24,759 --> 00:01:28,164
“Hand me the list,” he said.

14
00:01:28,164 --> 00:01:37,113
“I will check one entry.” They allowed him to isolate three errors in an hour, and not one of them was small.

15
00:01:37,113 --> 00:01:43,602
“Your idea is a conjunction,” he said, “not a sentence.”

16
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:51,216
A calculating visitor from the city office asked whether the project could be utilized by anyone else.

17
00:01:51,216 --> 00:01:55,785
“That is the whole reason it exists,” Maya said.

18
00:01:55,785 --> 00:02:10,048
“Keep the till open.” Her confident front concealed a real problem: an encounter with a nude statue in the art building had produced the best photograph of the year, and no data at all.

19
00:02:10,048 --> 00:02:13,793
“Put it on the wall anyway,” she said.

20
00:02:13,793 --> 00:02:21,347
They could see where the argument was going, and their very assumption that a number could settle it was the problem.

21
00:02:21,347 --> 00:02:26,926
Much of the analysis expected a clean answer, and none was there.

22
00:02:26,926 --> 00:02:40,818
The human relationship behind every number was the part no table could confirm; a herd of students in the corridor was not a relation, and a photograph was not a measurement.
